**Connection to my Module 2 findings:**  
Nuclear engineering connects with my interest in technology, science, math, and problem solving. It also fits my desire to have a career where the work has an important real-world purpose. I like that nuclear technology can be used in areas such as energy, medicine, research, national security, and space exploration.

**What excites me about this career:**  
What excites me most is the variety of things I could work on. I could potentially work with nuclear energy, reactor safety, medical technology, research, or even space-related nuclear systems. I also like that the career combines technical knowledge with solving difficult real-world problems.

**What concerns me or gives me pause:**  
My biggest concern is the difficulty of the math, physics, and engineering coursework required to earn the degree. I know I would have to become much stronger in those areas. However, I enjoy math and science much more when I understand what I am doing, so I think it is something I would be willing to work toward.

**My T-Shape for this career:**  
My deep area of expertise would be nuclear engineering, with a possible specialization in nuclear safety, reactor systems, or nuclear energy. My adjacent skills would include cybersecurity, computer programming, communication, data analysis, leadership, and systems analysis. Keeping some cybersecurity knowledge could be especially useful because modern nuclear facilities depend heavily on computer and control systems.

