## Reflection

I conducted some research on nuclear engineering, anesthesiology, and political science. I have a better understanding of what I want in a career and which careers fit my interests the best. Nuclear engineering stood out to me the most even before I did this activity because it combines math, science, problem-solving, and technology and has applications in big areas I am interested in like energy, medicine, and space exploration. I also found it interesting that nuclear engineers can work in all kinds of places whether it's a power plant, research laboratory, government agency, or engineering company, which gives me a lot of different options after getting a degree. Anesthesiology interested me because of my interest in medicine and the amount of critical thinking involved, but the long education and training process and the high level of responsibility made it way less appealing to me as a career. Political science was interesting because of the analysis side of the career, but I definitely did not feel nearly as connected to it compared to the other two careers. My RIASEC results were investigative, social, and enterprising which also made more sense after doing this research because nuclear engineering can involve technical problem solving while also requiring teamwork and communication. One thing that concerns me about nuclear engineering is that it will require me to become much stronger in math and science, but that also gives me something to work toward since I actually enjoy those subjects when I understand them. The research made me more confident that nuclear engineering is a career I should seriously pursue because it matches my interests while still giving me opportunities to work with people and eventually take on more responsibility. I also learned that I do not necessarily want a career that is only technical or only focused on people, but something that combines both, which is one of the main reasons nuclear engineering stood out to me.
